      "knowledge_point_id": "b6b720d4-59b0-5d86-b856-ff69abfe4152",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating Combinations Given Using Alternate Notation",
      "problem": "Evaluate $(\\frac{7}{6}).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that $(\\frac{7}{6})$ means the same thing as ${}_{7}{C}_{6}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ and $r=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{6} & =\\frac{7!}{(7-6)!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{1!6!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{1\\cdot \\cancel{6!}} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
