    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-84956",
      "knowledge_point_id": "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten",
      "problem": "A pizzeria has nine different toppings from which to choose. How many different pizzas can be made using only three toppings?",
      "worked_solution": "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ toppings, so $n=9.$ We want to select $3$ of those toppings for each of the pizzas, so we have $r=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different pizzas is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{9}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{9!}{(9-3)!\\,3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{6!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7\\cdot \\cancel{6!}}{\\cancel{6!}3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 8\\cdot 7}{3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{504}{6} \\\\  & =84.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
