  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931854",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-122551",
      "knowledge_point_id": "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten",
      "problem": "Mike wants to work out five days a week. In how many ways can Mike choose five days to work out?",
      "worked_solution": "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $7$ days in a week, so $n=7.$ We want to select $5$ of those days for Mike to workout, so we have $r=5.$\n\nTherefore, the number of choices is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{5} & =\\frac{7!}{(7-5)!\\,5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{2!5!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot \\cancel{5!}}{2!\\cancel{5!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{42}{2} \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
