      "math_academy_id": "q-1781",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten",
      "problem": "How many different ways can a group of four be made from a room of eighty people?",
      "worked_solution": "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $80$ people, so $n=80.$ We want to select $4$ of those people for each group, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential groups of four is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{80}{C}_{4} & =\\frac{80!}{(80-4)!\\,4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{80!}{76!4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\cdot 79\\cdot 78\\cdot 77\\cdot \\cancel{76!}}{\\cancel{76!}4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\cdot 79\\cdot 78\\cdot 77}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{37\\,957\\,920}{24} \\\\  & =1\\,581\\,580.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
