  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-124611",
      "knowledge_point_id": "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65",
      "knowledge_point": "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle",
      "problem": "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $A?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{5}{9}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $x=\\frac{2}{3}.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\frac{2}{9}",
              "option": "0",
