      "knowledge_point_id": "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/6c36dbce847c719accc77fa15f33dfb9a87cfae669691afe30f7693663076d2e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/52fe94bb303a001e89f9db221e9730d4ca8c5c284217aa20fb6084ee041dec66.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{10}{16}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{20}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
