  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65072",
      "knowledge_point_id": "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931905/assets/fc291f1fc5c4a712d3eef7ea2872de621855669d97445b24a2bc7a9da55949d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{4}{5})}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
